const DATA=[
['derrick','Derrick (K-type mast)','ZJ50 K-type derrick, about 45 m effective height, rated about 3,150 kN hook load. Open at the V-door side; carries the crown block, traveling block, top drive and the racked pipe stands.','Dropped objects, work at height, structural overload, lightning.','Fall-arrest harness with 100% tie-off, tool lanyards, drop-zone barricades, regular structural inspection, lightning stop-work rule.'],
['crown','Crown block','Fixed sheaves at the top of the mast; the drilling line runs from here to the traveling block.','Dropped objects, line or sheave failure, work at height.','Secure all fittings, scheduled inspection and lubrication, no overhead work while drilling.'],
['monkey','Monkey board (racking board)','Working platform about 27 m (90 ft) up the mast, bolted to the front legs. It is a frame with a narrow gangway and two OPEN finger racks, not a floor: stands pass up between the yellow fingers and their tops are held by the fingers. The escape post, trolley and line are at the front left.','Fall from height, dropped objects, pinch points from moving stands, wind and heat.','Harness with 100% tie-off, tool lanyards, handrails and toe boards, emergency escape device, secured finger latches, no unauthorised access.'],
['escapeline','Emergency escape line and trolley','Derrickman emergency escape system. A yellow post on the front left of the monkey board holds the end of the escape line on a black bracket. An orange trolley with two wheels rides on the line and carries the lanyard and handle. The line is hung slack (it sags in a gentle curve, not tight) and runs down about 70 m to an anchor post on the left side of the site, inside the fence, and ends in a spring buffer above a sand landing pit.','Fall from height when clipping in, trolley or line failure, overspeed on the descent, striking the anchor or buffer, people or equipment under the line, wear or corrosion that is not seen.','Inspect and load-test the line, trolley wheels, bracket, post and anchor on a schedule and keep the records; harness and lanyard checked before use; derrickmen trained and drilled; trolley parked and secured at the post; keep the line path and landing clear; no work under the line; buffer maintained.'],
['escapelanding','Escape line landing (sand pit)','Landing spot at the end of the escape line, on the left side of the site, inside the perimeter fence. A timber-framed pit filled with loose sand cushions the landing. Beyond it stand the anchor post on a concrete block with a back stay, and a spring buffer on the line. A green sign marks the area.','Hard landing or striking the anchor, sand that is packed down or full of stones, trip over the timber frame, sand blown or washed away, people or stored items left in the landing zone, snakes and animals in the sand.','Keep the sand loose, deep enough to cushion a landing and free of stones and debris; check it before drills and after rain or wind; keep the area clear and fenced from storage; inspect the buffer, post and stay; mark the route from the pit to the muster point; include the landing in escape drills.'],
['stands','Racked pipe stands','Stands of drill pipe stored vertically on the rig floor, their tops held in the fingers of the monkey board.','Falling or toppling stands, crush injuries, dropped objects, pinch points between pipe.','Closed finger latches, tied-back stand tops, tail ropes, keep clear of the racks, inspect fingers and latches.'],
['ladder','Caged ladders (derrickman)','Two caged ladders from the rig floor up the mast to the monkey board.','Fall from height, dropped objects on climbers, slips, hand injuries.','Cage and fall-arrest system, three points of contact, tools carried in a bag, inspect rungs and cage, clear the area below.'],
['block','Traveling block and hook','Sheave cluster that runs up and down the mast on the drilling line and carries the top drive below it.','Crush and pinch points, dropped objects, line breakage, over-hoisting into the crown.','Crown-saver limit switch, stay out of the red zone below the block, drill-line slip-and-cut program, lockout before maintenance.'],
["topdrive", "Top drive (AC, 500-ton class)", "The top drive replaces the kelly and rotary drive. From the top: the lifting bail (interlocked with the block hook) with the counterbalance cylinders; the gooseneck (S-pipe), which takes mud from the kelly hose into the wash pipe packing; two AC drilling motors with disc brakes and cooling blowers; the gearbox housing, which carries the load and turns the main shaft (quill); the link adapter and rotating pipe handler with link-tilt cylinders; the remote-controlled upper IBOP and the manual lower IBOP; the saver sub; and the backup tong (torque wrench) that holds the pipe while the motors make up or break out a connection. The red links and elevator pick up the next stand. The guide dolly keeps it on the rails and carries the torque reaction into the derrick. A top drive on a ZJ50-class rig is typically the DQ50 type (about 500-ton class); units of this class are typically about 5.5-6.5 m tall and weigh about 15-16 t.", "Rotating quill and entanglement, dropped objects from the unit, pinch points at the pipe handler and links, stored hydraulic and torque energy, high-pressure mud at the gooseneck, wash pipe and hose, high-voltage motors and cable, work at height when servicing.", "Interlocks between the elevator, links and IBOP, torque limits on make-up and break-out, lock-out and tag-out before maintenance, IBOP function tests, secondary retention on pins and bolts and drop checks, regular wash pipe and hose inspection, fall arrest and a permit for work in the derrick, guide dolly and rail inspection."],
['drawworks','Drawworks (1,200 kW class)','Main hoisting winch on the rig floor behind the rotary table. The drum spools the drilling line over the crown block to lift and lower the block and top drive; the brake holds and controls the load.','Brake failure or overspeed, line breakage, entanglement at the drum, noise, over-hoisting if limits fail.','Brake and auxiliary-brake tests each shift, crown-saver and weight-indicator checks, drilling-line slip-and-cut, fixed guards, driller stays at the brake while the load moves.'],
['floor','Rig floor and substructure','Raised work deck where the crew handles pipe. The substructure carries the load and raises the floor above the BOP.','Slips, trips and falls, falls from the edge, pinch points, noise.','Handrails and kick plates, non-slip deck, housekeeping, hearing protection, use the stairs only.'],
['cabin','Driller\'s cabin','Enclosed control cabin on the rig floor beside the drawworks. The driller runs the brake, top drive, pumps and well-control panel from here.','Noise, fatigue, missed alarms, impact from nearby equipment failure.','Alarm checks each shift, fatigue management, clear communication, emergency shut-down and well-control panel within reach.'],
['doghouse','Doghouses (white, outside the floor)','Two crew and tool shelters, one on each side, bolted to the edge of the rig floor outside its footprint and entered from the walkway.','Heat stress, congestion at the door, slips at the threshold, tripping over stored tools.','Keep doors and walkway clear, cooling and drinking water, housekeeping, no storage on the walkway.'],
['rotary','Rotary table and drill string','Turns and supports the drill pipe going down the hole; connections are made up here.','Entanglement in rotating parts, hand injuries, sudden torque release.','No loose clothing, guards on rotating parts, tongs and slips procedure, stop rotation before approaching.'],
['tongs','Manual tongs (make-up and break-out)','Two hanging manual tongs, one each side of the rotary table, used to make up and break out tool joints and to hold backup torque. Each hangs from the derrick on a cable over a sheave with a counterweight; a long handle is pulled by the crew or a cathead line, with a snub line to the derrick.','Caught-between and crush at the jaws and handle, tong swing and recoil, snapped tong or snub line, dies slipping off the pipe, hand and finger injuries.','Keep hands off the jaws and handle path, never stand in line with the handle or snub line, inspect dies, latch, cables and snub line, use a dedicated tong operator, stop work if a tong slips.'],
['bop','BOP stack','Blowout preventer stack on the wellhead, directly under the rotary table. From the bottom: wellhead (casing head), drilling spool, lower pipe ram, blind ram, upper pipe ram, annular preventer, then the bell nipple. The rams are the long horizontal red bodies, each with a hydraulic cylinder and a manual lock handwheel at both ends (blue cylinders = pipe rams, yellow = blind ram). The round blue body above them is the annular. Coloured hoses from the BOP control unit open and close each preventer.','Blowout, H2S or gas release, a preventer that fails to close, rams that do not match the pipe size in use, trapped pressure under a closed ram, high-pressure fluid, hydraulic injection from a hose, dropped objects.','Scheduled pressure and function tests, accumulator pre-charge checks, kick drills, ram sizes matched to the pipe in the hole, manual locks tested, control hoses and fittings inspected, trained well-control crew, gas detection, never work on a preventer that is under pressure.'],
['chokekill','Choke and kill lines (BOP outlets)','Two side outlets on the drilling spool, below the rams. Each has a manual gate valve next to the spool and a hydraulic (HCR) valve outside it. The choke line (red, to the right) carries the well flow to the choke manifold during well control. The kill line (blue, to the left) lets heavy mud be pumped into the well; it ends at a connection with a valve, a union cap and a pressure gauge for the mud pump or cement unit.','Line washout or leak under pressure, valves left in the wrong position, trapped pressure, unsecured line whipping, loose union, blocked or frozen line.','Valves lined up and function tested to the schedule, lines anchored and clamped, HCR valves tested from the control unit, valve position checklist, unions and gauge checked, keep clear of the lines when they are under pressure.'],
["shaker", "Shale shakers (2) and header box", "Primary solids control. The header box distributes the mud to two linear-motion shakers. Vibrating screens let the mud and fine solids fall through into the sand trap, while coarse cuttings travel up the inclined screen and slide down the discharge trough straight into the mud disposal pit. The cut point depends on the screen mesh; a 200-mesh screen cuts at about 74 microns. Screens are changed when they blind or tear.", "Noise above 85 dB, vibrating and moving parts, hot oily mud and vapours (benzene, H2S in sour wells), slips on cuttings, hand injuries when changing screens, electrical hazards.", "Guards and interlocks, lock-out before screen changes, hearing protection, gloves and eye protection, vapour extraction and gas detection, housekeeping, check screens for holes."],
["tank1", "Tank 1 - Shaker tank", "The stair at the shaker end ends on a landing that crosses over the flare and vent lines on the ground, with steps down on the far side, so nobody has to climb over the pipes. First tank in the line, on the right stack next to the pit. Compartment 1 is the sand trap under the shakers: no agitator and a sloped bottom with a dump valve, so heavy solids that passed the screens settle and are dumped through the dump line into the pit. Compartment 2 is the degasser suction. Compartment 3 receives the degassed mud and is the desander suction. Weirs between the compartments let the mud overflow from one to the next. A grated walkway extension on the pit side runs along all three tanks of the right stack, with handrails and kick plates, and cross bridges and an aisle walkway between the two stacks link the left stack (tanks 4 and 5). The crew can walk the whole mud system; the shaker, desander, desilter and centrifuge chutes run to the mud pit underneath the walkway.", "Falls from the roof (2.6 m), confined space entry for cleaning, H2S and hydrocarbon vapour in the headspace, slips on grating, overflow.", "Handrails on all open edges, two exits per stack (stairs at both ends), confined space permit with gas test and standby person, level alarms, keep the grating clear."],
["degasser", "Vacuum degasser", "A vacuum pump draws mud from compartment 2 into a chamber where it spreads in a thin film so that entrained gas bubbles break out. The gas leaves through its own vent line; the degassed mud is discharged into compartment 3. It removes small entrained gas that lowers the mud density and pump efficiency. It is not designed for large gas influxes, which go to the gas buster.", "Flammable or toxic gas at the vent, rotating vacuum pump and motor, electrical hazard.", "Vent line to a safe location, gas detection, guarded couplings, lock-out before maintenance, check suction and discharge for blockage."],
["tank2", "Tank 2 - Cleaning tank", "Compartment 4 receives the desander overflow and is the desilter suction. Compartment 5 receives the desilter overflow and is the centrifuge feed. Compartment 6 receives the centrifuge liquid and holds the cleaned mud, which then passes to tank 3. Each compartment has an agitator to keep solids in suspension. Underflow troughs from the cones and the centrifuge run east over the tank wall to the pit.", "Falls from the roof, confined space, entanglement at agitator shafts, vapour in the headspace, overflow.", "Handrails, agitator guards and lock-out, confined space permit, level alarms, gas detection."],
["desander", "Desander (2 cones)", "A centrifugal pump takes mud from compartment 3 and feeds it tangentially into large hydrocyclone cones (typically 10-12 in). Heavy particles spin to the wall and leave at the bottom as underflow (sand), which runs down the trough to the mud disposal pit. The cleaned mud leaves at the top as overflow and flows into compartment 4. Typical cut point about 50-75 microns.", "High-pressure feed lines, erosion of cones and pump parts, solids discharge, noise, electrical hazard.", "Feed pressure gauge and correct head, inspect cones for wear, secured piping, pump guards, lock-out before maintenance."],
["desilter", "Desilter (8 cones)", "The same principle with smaller cones (typically 4 in) fed by its own pump from compartment 4. It removes silt-size solids, about 15-40 microns; its underflow runs down the trough to the pit and its overflow goes into compartment 5. In weighted mud its underflow also contains barite, so a desilter is normally bypassed in favour of a mud cleaner or centrifuge.", "High-pressure feed lines, wear of cones, solids discharge, noise, electrical hazard.", "Pressure gauge and correct feed head, inspect cones, secured piping and guards, lock-out before maintenance."],
["centrifuge", "Decanter centrifuge", "A horizontal rotating bowl (typically about 2,000-4,000 rpm) with a screw conveyor inside. The feed pump takes mud from compartment 5. Heavy solids are thrown to the bowl wall and conveyed to the solids discharge, which drops into a chute to the mud disposal pit; the liquid leaves through the liquid discharge into compartment 6. It removes the finest solids, down to about 2-5 microns. With weighted mud it is run to recover barite and discard the fine fraction.", "High-speed rotating bowl, vibration, noise, pinch points at the belt guard, electrical hazard, hot surfaces.", "Never open covers until the bowl has stopped completely, vibration and torque trips, lock-out, guards, hearing protection, wash down after use."],
["tank3", "Tank 3 - Clean mud tank (storage)", "Last tank of the right stack. It receives the cleaned mud from tank 2 and stores it. It is joined to tank 5, the active tank, by equaliser lines with valves, so clean mud can be transferred across to the active system.", "Falls from the roof, confined space, entanglement at agitator shafts, overflow when transferring.", "Handrails and stair, agitator guards, valve line-up check before any transfer, level alarms, confined space permit."],
["tank4", "Tank 4 - Reserve tank (storage)", "Left stack. Holds pre-mixed spare mud, for example higher-density mud and lost-circulation material, ready to be transferred to the active system through the equaliser valves. Agitated to keep barite in suspension, with level monitoring. Normally isolated from the active line.", "Falls from the roof, confined space, entanglement at agitator shafts, overflow when transferring.", "Handrails and stair, agitator guards, valve line-up check before any transfer, level alarms, confined space permit."],
["tank5", "Tank 5 - Active and mixing tank (storage)", "Left stack, beside the mud pumps. Compartment 1 of this tank is the mixing compartment with the mixing hopper and mixing pump; compartment 2 is the active compartment from which both mud pumps draw. Pit-level sensors (pit volume totalizer) monitor the volume to detect kicks and losses.", "Dust and chemicals at the hopper, falls from the roof, entanglement at agitator shafts, confined space.", "Handrails, agitator guards, confined space permit, level alarms, PPE and eyewash at the hopper."],
["hopper", "Mixing hopper and mixing pump", "A funnel (jet venturi hopper) where powders such as bentonite, barite and chemicals are added. A centrifugal mixing pump forces mud through a venturi nozzle under the hopper; the low pressure sucks the powder in and mixes it into the stream that returns to tank 5. An eyewash station stands next to it.", "Dust inhalation (barite, bentonite, caustic soda), chemical burns and eye injuries, manual handling of sacks, spills.", "Respirator or dust mask, goggles, gloves, eyewash station, safety data sheets, mechanical sack handling, careful housekeeping."],
["rack", "Pipe racks and catwalk", "The V-door ramp carries pipe up to the rig floor, and the long catwalk with its dark centre slot feeds it. On each side lie drill pipe stacks: two ground stacks nearest the rig on wooden sleepers with straps, and two raised steel racks that stand at the edge of the pad, reached by forklift or crane. The pipe lies in layers; every joint has a steel tool joint and a coloured thread protector on both ends, which stay on until the pipe is picked up.", "Rolling pipe, crush injuries, falls from the raised racks, strains, dropped loads, slips on the catwalk, vehicle and crane movement.", "Chock and strap every stack, stack no higher than the rack stops, exclusion zone at the ramp and catwalk, mechanical handling with tag lines, thread protectors kept on, trained signalman for lifts."],
["flowline", "Bell nipple and flowline", "The bell nipple sits on top of the BOP stack under the rotary table and funnels the returning mud into a side outlet. The flowline carries it by gravity, sloping continuously downward, to the header box (possum belly) above the shale shakers. The returning mud carries cuttings, any formation gas and, in sour wells, H2S.", "Gas and H2S released at the header box, blocked or washed-out flowline, spills, flow-out surging when the well kicks.", "Continuous gas and H2S detection with alarms, flow-out (paddle) sensor on the flowline, continuous slope with supports, secured connections, inspection each tour, shut-in per the well-control procedure."],
["choke", "Choke manifold", "The choke line from the BOP enters the manifold during well control. Three parallel runs are available: a manual (adjustable) choke, a bypass, and a hydraulic (remote) choke. Each has gate valves upstream and downstream, and the headers carry pressure gauges. Flow is routed out to the mud-gas separator, and a second outlet leads to the flare line. Pressures here are the well pressures, so the manifold is built and rated for the BOP stack rating.", "High-pressure leaks and washouts, erosion of the choke, sudden pressure surge, gas release, noise.", "Pressure test to the BOP rating, correct valve line-up checked each tour, choke and valve function tests, gauges calibrated, exclusion zone when circulating a kick, gas detection, secured lines."],
["gasbuster", "Mud-gas separator (gas buster)", "A vertical vessel with internal baffle plates and a U-tube liquid mud seal at the bottom, placed south of tank 1 as the separator on the plan. During well control gas-cut mud comes from the choke manifold into the vessel. Gas rises and leaves through the vent line, which drops to the ground beside the vessel and lies on the ground, beside the mud disposal pit, to the flare pit outside the rig site. The degassed mud leaves through the mud seal to the header box. A second blowdown line runs alongside the vent line to the flare pit. In normal drilling mud goes straight to the shakers.", "Loss of the mud seal lets gas into the shaker area, over-pressure of the vessel, flammable and toxic gas at the vent, liquid carry-over to the flare pit, fire.", "Keep the mud seal full and sized for the expected pressure, vent line anchored on sleepers, short and straight, no valves in it and led to the flare pit at a safe distance downwind, gas detectors, no ignition sources, inspect and pressure test."],
["pumps", "Mud pumps (2 triplex) and suction manifold", "Two triplex reciprocating pumps with electric drive, west of the left tank stack beside tank 5. The suction manifold on the tank wall feeds both pumps through butterfly valves. The pumps pressurise clean mud and send it to the pump manifold, then to the standpipe, kelly hose and top drive. Each has a pulsation dampener (blue sphere) and a relief or pop-off valve (red). Typical class: 1,300-1,600 hp and up to about 5,000 psi.", "High-pressure leaks (injection injury), pipe whip, pulsation, rotating coupling, noise, hot surfaces, electrical.", "Relief valve set below the rating of the weakest component and piped to the tank, dampener pre-charge checked, guarded couplings, lock-out and bleed pressure before work, pressure-test lines, exclusion zone while pumping."],
["pumpmanifold", "Pump manifold (discharge)", "Collects the discharge of both pumps through a check valve and a plug valve per pump into one common header. The header carries a pressure transducer and dial gauge, a relief valve and a bleed-off valve that both drain to the mud tank, and the line that runs to the standpipe at the rig. The check valves stop mud from flowing back into a stopped pump, and the plug valves isolate each pump for maintenance.", "High-pressure release when opening a valve, pipe whip, injection injury, wrong valve line-up against a closed system.", "Valve line-up checklist, bleed pressure through the bleed-off valve before work, relief valve set and tested, lock-out and tag-out, pressure test after maintenance, exclusion zone while pumping."],
["pit", "Mud disposal pit (waste from all solids control)", "A wide, lined, bunded pit on the right side of the tank area. All solids waste drains here by gravity: cuttings from the shale shakers, sand from the sand trap dump line, the desander and desilter underflow, and the centrifuge solids. The liner (HDPE geomembrane) sits on a prepared base inside an earth berm, with a fence, danger signs and a rescue ring. The waste is later removed by vacuum truck or treated and disposed of according to the waste management plan and local regulations. In this model the pit is drawn as a bunded pit above ground level.", "Drowning or engulfment, hydrocarbon and H2S vapour, liner damage and seepage to soil and groundwater, overtopping after rain, animals falling in, vehicle collisions at the berm.", "Fence with signs and a controlled gate, rescue ring and rope, freeboard checks, liner and berm inspection, no entry without a permit, wildlife protection, vacuum-truck procedures, gas detection when working near it."],
["flarepit", "Flare pit and vent lines", "The gas buster vent line and a second blowdown line lie on the ground on sleepers beside the mud disposal pit and leave the rig site to a flare pit. Gas from gas-cut mud is burned there, far from the well and the tanks. Steel arch covers protect the lines where vehicles drive over them. The pit has a bunded wall, a fence, a danger sign and burner risers with a pilot.", "Radiant heat, open flame as an ignition source, unburnt gas and H2S, liquid carry-over from the blowdown line, brush fire, vehicles crossing the lines.", "Position downwind and far from the well and tanks, exclusion fence, cleared firebreak, pilot and ignition checks, line supports and crossing covers, knock out liquids before the pit, gas detection."],
["mudlogging", "Mud logging unit", "A container cabin where the mud logger monitors the well. It holds the data acquisition computers and displays (rate of penetration, weight on bit, pump pressure, pit volumes), the gas detection and gas chromatograph equipment that measure gas in the returning mud, and a bench with a microscope and a sample catcher for examining cuttings. It sits near the shakers so samples can be collected quickly; the roof AC unit and antenna mast are visible.", "Flammable gas near electrical equipment, H2S exposure when collecting samples at the shakers, slips on the stairs, fire, electrical hazards.", "Electrical equipment rated for the hazardous area, gas detectors and alarms, fire extinguishers, stair handrails, H2S training and escape sets, earthing and bonding, emergency exits kept clear."],
["mudlab", "Mud lab", "A container laboratory where the mud engineer tests the mud properties several times a shift: density with a mud balance, viscosity with a Marsh funnel and a rotational viscometer, fluid loss with a filter press, sand content, pH and chlorides, and solids and oil/water ratio with a retort. The results decide the chemical additions made at the mixing hopper. The roof AC unit and antenna mast are visible.", "Chemical splashes and dust, hot retort surfaces, glass breakage, electrical hazards, slips, fire.", "Safety data sheets, gloves and eye protection, eyewash and first-aid kit, fume extraction, waste chemicals stored and disposed of correctly, fire extinguishers, housekeeping."],
["powerhouse", "Power house: generators + compressors", "One steel building behind the rig that holds both diesel generator sets and the air compressor plant. Each generator set stands on a skid with its own fuel base tank: an in-line six-cylinder diesel with turbocharger, an alternator with terminal box, a radiator and fan that blow through a duct to the west wall louvres, a control panel with emergency stop, and a roof exhaust with bellows, silencer, spark arrestor and rain cap. Air enters through louvres in the long walls. The east bay holds two screw compressors, a refrigerated air dryer and a lube-oil drum pallet; the wet and dry air receivers stand outside the south wall. A monorail beam with a chain hoist covers each generator for engine work. Output cables leave through floor trenches and gland plates into covered ground trays that run to the VFD room. Press Tank cutaway to see the equipment inside.", "Ignition source near the well area, electric shock and arc flash, diesel and lube-oil fire, noise above 85 dB, exhaust gas and carbon monoxide, hot surfaces and exhaust stacks, rotating fans and couplings, stored compressed air, fuel spills when refuelling, lifting loads under the hoist.", "Check the building position against the site hazardous-area drawing and its distance from the wellhead, gas detection at the intake louvres with generator shutdown, spark arrestors on every exhaust, emergency stops at the door and on each set, fire detection and extinguishers, earthing and bonding of the tanker at the fill points, lock-out and tag-out, hearing protection, carbon monoxide detection, guarded fans and couplings, receiver relief valves tested, hoist inspected and marked with its safe working load."],
["vfd", "VFD room (drives)", "The variable frequency drive (VFD) cabinets that control the AC motors on the drawworks, top drive and rotary table, with MCC and PLC cabinets, a transformer, and braking resistors on the roof. Two through-wall air conditioners keep the drive electronics cool. The room stands beside the power house. Power arrives from the generators through gland plates in the wall that faces the power house and leaves to the rig floor and to the BOP control room through gland plates in the end wall that faces the rig, in covered cable trays that lie on the ground. The Tank cutaway button shows the cabinet rows inside.", "Electric shock, arc flash, energy stored in the drive capacitors, fire, overheating, blocked exits.", "Arc-flash rated PPE and labels, lock-out and wait for the capacitors to discharge, door interlocks, air conditioning with a high-temperature alarm, fire detection, clear exits, trained electricians only."],
["cables", "Covered ground cable trays", "Steel cable trays that lie directly on the ground between the power house, the VFD room, the BOP control room and the rig, each closed by short bolted yellow lids. Heavy generator power cables run in separate trays from the control and signal cables. Joints are bridged with copper bonding straps, concrete sleepers keep the trays off wet sand, and heavy rubber ramps let people and vehicles cross. At the back of the rig substructure two covered vertical risers, braced to the floor edge and protected by crash bollards, climb to a junction box on the rig floor.", "Trip and vehicle crush, water and sand getting in, cable damage and arc fault, overheating when buried under debris, power and signal cables mixed, shock when a lid is opened.", "Ramps at every crossing, crash bollards at the riser, power and signal kept apart, bonding at every joint, lids bolted and latched, lock-out before opening, inspect after vehicle movements, route marker posts, cable size derated for desert heat."],
["koomey", "BOP control room (Koomey unit)", "Houses the accumulator unit that powers the blowout preventers. Accumulator bottles pre-charged with nitrogen hold hydraulic fluid under pressure (typically 3,000 psi); electric and air-driven pumps recharge them. A fluid reservoir and the control manifold, with regulators and gauges, operate the rams and the annular preventer. A hose bundle runs along the ground and up to the BOP stack, and remote panels on the rig floor operate the same functions. The room stands well clear of the well (typically at least about 15 m) so it can be reached in an emergency.", "Stored hydraulic and nitrogen energy, high-pressure hose failure, failure to operate when needed, fire damage to the hoses.", "Regular function tests of every preventer, pre-charge and capacity checks, protected (fire-sleeved) hose bundle, remote panels checked, clear access to the room, nothing stored inside."],
['standpipe','Standpipe and kelly hose','High-pressure pipe carrying drilling mud from the pumps up the mast, then a flexible kelly hose to the top drive gooseneck.','High-pressure leak (injection injury), whipping hose, burst, hose chafing as the top drive moves.','Pressure testing, safety clamps and hose restraints, pump relief valve, exclusion zone while pumping at pressure.'],
['oilstore','Diesel storage containers (2) with bund','Two containerised diesel and lubricant storage units at the edge of the pad, away from the well and the power house. Each has corrugated steel walls, a side door and end doors, and a roof deck with handrails reached by a ladder. They stand together inside a lined concrete bund that holds any spill, with a locked rain drain, a flameproof transfer pump with filter, meter and hose reel, a static earthing point, extinguishers, a spill kit, warning signs and crash bollards.','Fire and explosion from flammable vapour, spills and soil contamination, falls from the roof deck, slips on the ladder and the bund steps, static discharge when transferring, vehicle impact.','Bund sized for the largest container and kept dry, rain drain closed and locked, earth the tanker and the hose before transfer, no ignition sources without a hot-work permit, extinguishers and spill kits, gas test before entry, fall protection and three-point contact on the ladder, crash bollards, doors kept locked.'],
["entrance", "Duty room / security cabin", "The white duty room (guard cabin) stands on the right side of the well site gate, with a window, a roof air conditioner and a yellow base stripe, next to the muster point. Two orange and white windsocks and a tall corner flood-light pole stand on the pad edge. Every person and vehicle stops at the gate to be checked, inducted and logged before entering the pad.", "Vehicle and pedestrian conflict, barrier arm strike, unauthorised entry, poor lighting at night, heat for the guard.", "Stop and report at the barrier, induction and PPE check, visitor and vehicle log, barrier kept in working order, lighting, guard trained in emergency procedures."],
["gate", "Standard well site gate", "Two 0.7 m steel lattice columns (3.5 m with the base) on yellow and black hazard bases, a 6 m clear opening (not less than 4.5 m) for vehicles, and a red and white boom barrier with a STOP sign that lifts when someone approaches. Left column front: company board, Non-Staff No Entry, Protective Equipment Must Be Worn and the H2S warning sign. Right column front: well name board, No Smoking or Open Flame, and the Step-Back Five Steps work method. The back faces carry the company slogans. Built after the Standardized Drilling Site Demonstration Atlas (Edition D), page 05.", "Vehicle strike on the columns, barrier arm strike, unauthorised entry, entering without PPE or without knowing the H2S rules, queuing on the road.", "Base at least 20 cm high and level, clear opening at least 4.5 m, signs on both sides, the barrier stays closed until the visitor is registered, everyone stops at the desk first."],
["fence", "Perimeter fence", "A 2.3 m chain-link fence runs around the whole well site. Posts stand every 3 m with a Y arm on top that carries razor-wire coils. The only opening is the main gate. The escape line landing, the muster area, the camp and all the equipment are inside the fence.", "Cuts from the razor wire, fence damaged or cut and not repaired, vehicles hitting the fence, blocking the escape route, climbing over the fence, people locked in or out in an emergency.", "Inspect the fence and razor wire on a schedule and repair damage at once; keep the line clear on both sides; keep the gate and the muster route open and unobstructed; no climbing or passing material over the fence; wear gloves when working near the wire."],
["fivesigns", "Five Signs and One Map + registration desk", "Beside the road, in order from outside to inside: the entry registration desk (stainless, umbrella), the six boards (Construction Notice, Environmental Information Notice Board, Main Risk Notification, Well Site Layout and Escape Diagram, Anti-Violation Prohibitions and Life-Saving Clauses, Well Site Personnel Assembly Card), the Emergency Assembly Point sign and the hand-crank alarm. Each board is a stainless frame 1.0 m wide with a 1.5 m panel, 2.0 m overall; the personnel card board holds a card for each person who enters and is turned to Left Site when they leave. Atlas pages 06 to 08.", "Entering without reading the risks, not knowing the escape route, missing people at the headcount, the hand-crank alarm not working, boards unreadable or out of date.", "Read the boards before entering, post your assembly card, flip it when you leave, check the layout and escape diagram, test the alarm, keep the boards clean and current."],
["escapeslide", "Emergency escape slide", "The slide mouth is closed only by a safety chain across the rig-floor handrail, with nothing in the way: in walk mode, stepping over the chain sends the guide down the chute with no control until the landing cushion. A yellow enclosed-side escape chute that leaves the rig floor from a gated landing on the left face of the floor, with no ground supports, and runs down to a cushioned soft inflated landing cushion on the ground. It is the quick exit for the crew when the stairs are blocked by fire, gas or a well-control event. The cushion leads to the assembly path toward the muster point.", "Fall from the landing, blocked chute or run-out, injury on landing, misuse, damage or corrosion unseen, unclear route from the run-out.", "Gate kept chained but not locked, chute and run-out kept clear and lit, regular inspection and load test, signs at the top and the run-out, drills so every crew member has used it, route from the run-out to the muster point kept clear."],
["workshopa", "Workshop 1 (store and work container)", "A cream corrugated container workshop on timber sleepers, with a personnel door at one end, a large roller shutter door under a brown canopy, a roof air conditioner, wall-mounted split units at the back, a fire extinguisher and a waste bin at the door.", "Electric shock, fire, hand tools and moving parts, manual handling, slips at the step, heat in the container, carbon monoxide if engines run inside.", "Extinguisher at the door, earthing and RCD on the supply, guards on machines, ventilation and air conditioning, housekeeping, door kept clear, hot-work permit for welding."],
["workshopb", "Workshop 2 (welding and gas container)", "A grey corrugated container workshop on timber sleepers with a roof exhaust chimney, a personnel door, a rack of gas cylinders beside the door, a waste bin, an electrical box on the end wall, and roof and wall air conditioners.", "Cylinder fire and explosion, leaks of gas, welding fumes and arc flash, shock, heavy cylinders falling, noise.", "Cylinders upright, chained and capped, flashback arrestors and hoses checked, flammable and oxygen kept apart, fume extraction through the chimney, hot-work permit, welding PPE, extinguisher at the door."],
["chemstore", "Chemical storage area", "A closed storage building with walls on all sides, a flat blue roof, a roller shutter door and a personnel door, a bunded concrete floor, an eyewash station, a spill kit, extinguishers and a drum stand outside. It is a closed building with walls all round.", "Chemical spills and contact burns, fire, toxic fumes, incompatible chemicals stored together, drums falling, unauthorised access.", "Safety data sheets at the entrance, segregation by hazard class, bund and spill kit, eyewash tested weekly, correct labels, locked gate with a register, extinguishers, trained handlers."],
["secpost0", "Security post 1 (corner)", "A small guard booth at a corner of the rig site pad, with a window on two sides, a roof beacon and a barrier arm.", "Lone working, heat, vehicles at the barrier, intruders.", "Radio check every hour, air conditioning or shade, lighting, clear visitor and vehicle log."],
["secpost1", "Security post 2 (corner)", "A small guard booth at a corner of the rig site pad, with a window on two sides, a roof beacon and a barrier arm.", "Lone working, heat, vehicles at the barrier, intruders.", "Radio check every hour, air conditioning or shade, lighting, clear visitor and vehicle log."],
["secpost2", "Security post 3 (corner)", "A small guard booth at a corner of the rig site pad, with a window on two sides, a roof beacon and a barrier arm.", "Lone working, heat, vehicles at the barrier, intruders.", "Radio check every hour, air conditioning or shade, lighting, clear visitor and vehicle log."],
["secpost3", "Security post 4 (corner)", "A small guard booth at a corner of the rig site pad, with a window on two sides, a roof beacon and a barrier arm.", "Lone working, heat, vehicles at the barrier, intruders.", "Radio check every hour, air conditioning or shade, lighting, clear visitor and vehicle log."],
['camp','Accommodation trailers (3)','Three living trailers in a row on the left edge of the pad, each with a corrugated body, windows with louvres, roof air-conditioners, and steel entrance decks with canopies and stairs. A diesel tank and electrical cabinets sit in the gaps between them.','Fire in sleeping quarters, electrical faults, slips and falls on stairs and decks, heat stress, vehicle traffic, poor evacuation access.','Smoke and fire detection, extinguishers at every door, handrails and non-slip treads, earthing and RCD protection, clear escape routes to the muster point, shade and cooling, speed limits.'],
["cementsilo","Cement silos (4, 2 x 2 bay)","Four bulk cement silos in a 2 x 2 grid on a kerbed concrete slab inside a fence, used to store cement (or barite) for the cementing unit. Each silo is a welded steel cylinder with a conical hopper bottom on a braced four-leg frame, about 8.5 m to the roof rail, holding roughly 40-60 tonnes. Bulk trucks blow the powder in through the fill line and camlock coupling; a filter and a relief valve on the roof vent the displaced air; a caged ladder climbs to the roof walkway with its handrail; a butterfly valve and discharge outlet below the cone feed the cement unit. The fence has a gate on the south side for bulk trucks.","Cement dust inhalation, over-pressurising a silo while filling, bridged or blocked cement, falls from the ladder and the roof walkway, engulfment if anyone enters a silo, hose whip at a coupling, collapse of a leg or bracing, dropped objects during crane lifts, slips on cement dust.","Check the relief valve and roof filter, limit the fill pressure with a trained operator at the truck, dust mask and goggles, ladder cage and fall arrest with a permit for work at height, no entry into a silo without a confined-space permit and lock-out, whip checks and locked camlocks, inspect legs and bracing, keep the bay fenced and clear, banksman for all crane lifts."],
["crane","Mobile crane (truck-mounted)","A truck-mounted telescopic boom crane of about the 50-tonne class, parked southwest of the rig, west of the pipe catwalk. It is used to rig up and rig down, to move pipe and equipment and to lift loads such as cement unit parts and pipe. Four hydraulic outriggers on pads and timber cribbing level and stabilise it. The superstructure turns on a bearing with the operator cab on the side, the counterweight at the back and the boom raised on a luffing cylinder; the hook block hangs from the boom head on the hoist rope.","Overturning on soft ground or with outriggers not fully extended, overload, dropped load, people under the load or the boom, crushing between the counterweight and fixed objects, contact with the silos or an overhead line, high wind, outrigger pad sinking in sand.","Certified operator and rigger, written lift plan and load chart, outriggers fully extended on cribbing and the crane levelled, inspect the rope, hook latch and limit switches each day, banksman and tag lines, exclusion zone under the load and around the swing radius, stop lifting above the wind limit, never travel with a load, keep clear of the silo ladders during a lift."],
['muster','Muster point','Designated safe assembly area for headcount in an emergency.','Overcrowding, wrong position relative to wind, blocked access.','Upwind of the rig, visible sign and lighting, regular drills, headcount by supervisor, two access routes.'],
['wind','Windsock','Shows wind direction so the crew knows which way to evacuate after a gas release.','Being ignored or not visible during an H2S event.','Check at start of shift, evacuate crosswind then upwind, keep visible from the rig floor.'],
['crew','Rig crew','Workers wearing the required PPE.','Heat stress, dehydration, sun exposure, fatigue.','Hard hat, FR coveralls, gloves, safety glasses, boots; water, shade and work-rest cycles in desert heat.']];
